menuconfig MCUHOME
	bool "MCUHome firmware framework"
	help
	  Enable the MCUHome runtime: the component framework, the CoAP and
	  Matter networking layers and the device services generated from a
	  MCUHome YAML device configuration.

if MCUHOME

rsource "components/matter/Kconfig"
rsource "components/sensor/Kconfig"
rsource "drivers/entropy/Kconfig"
rsource "lib/debug/Kconfig"
rsource "lib/health/Kconfig"

# Further component and driver Kconfig trees are sourced here as they
# are implemented.

endif # MCUHOME

# Deliberately OUTSIDE the MCUHOME guard: this symbol is for the MCUboot
# image of a sysbuild, which builds with the mcuhome module present but
# CONFIG_MCUHOME off — the bootloader wants none of the runtime, only
# this one piece of glue.
config MCUHOME_BOOT_WATCHDOG
	bool "Arm the hardware watchdog from early bootloader init"
	depends on WATCHDOG
	help
	  MCUHome glue for the MCUboot image (lib/boot_watchdog/): arms the
	  hardware watchdog on the watchdog0 devicetree alias at
	  PRE_KERNEL_2, earlier than MCUboot's own arming in main(), and
	  overrides MCUboot's weak mcuboot_watchdog_setup() so that the
	  arming happens exactly once and with the options MCUHome chose
	  (MCUHOME_BOOT_WATCHDOG_PAUSE_ON_DEBUG below).

	  Why the bootloader needs its own watchdog: a fault in MCUboot ends
	  in Zephyr's default fatal handler, which HALTS. During a swap that
	  is a device with the application half-erased, spinning in a fault
	  handler with nothing left to reset it — observed live on the
	  bench, 2026-08-08, for over 1.5 hours until a manual reset. The
	  watchdog MCUHome's health design mandates for the application
	  (ADR 0015 health amendment) has to cover the minutes the
	  bootloader owns the SoC too, or every over-the-air update carries
	  an unguarded window exactly where a hang costs the most.

	  Set by the generated sysbuild/mcuboot.conf of every MCUHome build.
	  MCUboot's own swap loops and its serial-recovery loop feed the
	  watchdog (MCUBOOT_WATCHDOG_FEED); the application takes over
	  feeding via lib/health once it is up.

config MCUHOME_BOOT_WATCHDOG_PAUSE_ON_DEBUG
	bool "Pause the bootloader watchdog while the CPU is halted by a debugger"
	depends on MCUHOME_BOOT_WATCHDOG
	default n
	help
	  The bootloader-side twin of CONFIG_MCUHOME_WATCHDOG_PAUSE_ON_DEBUG
	  (lib/health/Kconfig), off for the same reason: a watchdog a stale
	  debug connection can silently disable is not a backstop.

	  This one decides for the whole boot chain, not only for MCUboot. On
	  nRF hardware the watchdog configuration is write-locked from the
	  moment it starts, so whatever the bootloader arms is what the
	  application inherits — a bench session that wants breakpoints has
	  to set this symbol as well as the application's, and setting only
	  the application's changes nothing at all.
